tous hypercalcemia (strong hypophosphatemia is more typical), but antacid use alone doesn't confirm this mechanism."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of antacid use, hypercalcemia, and low phosphorus (2.0 mg/dL) support milk-alkali syndrome as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Milk-alkali syndrome typically requires calcium and vitamin D intake, not just antacids. The absence of thiazide use or immobilization history further weakens this possibility."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of abdominal pain and trouble focusing with normal phosphorus (2.0 mg/dL) argue against PTH-mediated hypercalcemia in favor of a different mechanism?", "summary": "Normal phosphorus is atypical for PTH-mediated hypercalcemia (which usually causes hypophosphatemia), suggesting this mechanism is less likely while keeping other possibilities open."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of bone pain or elevated PTHrP in the lab results disfavor malignancy-associated hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Lack of bone pain and no mention of PTHrP reduces support for malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, though constitutional symptoms remain concerning."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the low serum phosphorus (2.0 mg/dL) contradict vitamin D/granulomatous hypercalcemia mechanisms?", "summary": "Low phosphorus is inconsistent with vitamin D/granulomatous hypercalcemia (which typically causes hyperphosphatemia), providing moderate evidence against this branch."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of thiazide use or immobilization history disfavor 'other hypercalcemic disorders' like milk-alkali syndrome?", "summary": "Classic milk-alkali syndrome requires calcium/vitamin D excess, not just antacids. Absence of thiazides/immobilization provides weak evidence against this category."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.697], ["B1.1", 0.