vs Parathyroid Adenoma:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, bone fractures, calcium nephrolithiasis, cardiac arrhythmia, depression\n\nMalignancy-associated Hypercalcemia (PTHrP/osteolytic) vs Parathyroid Hyperplasia:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: elevated circulating parathyroid hormone level, episodic abdominal pain, headache, hypercalciuria, nausea and vomiting\n\nMalignancy-associated Hypercalcemia (PTHrP/osteolytic) vs Parathyroid Carcinoma:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: difficulty breathing, difficulty swallowing, frequent loose bowel movements, hoarseness, neck lump\n\nParathyroid Adenoma vs Parathyroid Hyperplasia:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, bone fractures, calcium nephrolithiasis, cardiac arrhythmia, confusion\n  Favours second: dysphagia, episodic abdominal pain, headache, nausea and vomiting, nephrolithiasis\n\nParathyroid Adenoma vs Parathyroid Carcinoma:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, bone fractures, bone pain, calcium nephrolithiasis, cardiac arrhythmia\n  Favours second: difficulty breathing, difficulty swallowing, frequent loose bowel movements, hoarseness, neck lump\n\nParathyroid Hyperplasia vs Parathyroid Carcinoma:\n  Favours first:  bone pain, dysphagia, elevated circulating parathyroid hormone level, episodic abdominal pain, fatigue\n  Favours second: difficulty breathing, difficulty swallowing, frequent loose bowel movements, hoarseness, neck lump\n\nRelated to Malignancy-associated Hypercalcemia (PTHrP/osteolytic): hypercalcemia disease, 